$ lazysubmodules add -h
Usage: lazysubmodules add <url> <path> (--branch B | --tag T | --tag-pattern P | --commit SHA)

Clone a repository with 'git submodule add' as a new submodule named after
its path, write its tracking configuration, check out the resolved commit,
write .lsm.lock and stage the result. Exactly one of --branch, --tag,
--tag-pattern and --commit is required. If a step after the clone fails,
the new submodule is removed again.

Options:
  --branch <branch>        track the tip of the remote branch
  --commit <commit>        track the commit (a full or abbreviated SHA)
  --include-prerelease     let a tag pattern select a pre-release tag
  --tag <tag>              track the tag
  --tag-pattern <pattern>  track the highest version tag matching pattern

$ lazysubmodules set -h
Usage: lazysubmodules set <name> (--branch B | --tag T | --tag-pattern P | --commit SHA)

Change the tracking configuration of a submodule in .gitmodules, and nothing
else; run 'update' afterwards. An unmanaged submodule becomes managed.
Exactly one of --branch, --tag, --tag-pattern and --commit is required;
an abbreviated commit is expanded when it is available locally.

Options:
  --branch <branch>        track the tip of the remote branch
  --commit <commit>        track the commit (a full or abbreviated SHA)
  --tag <tag>              track the tag
  --tag-pattern <pattern>  track the highest version tag matching pattern

$ lazysubmodules update -h
Usage: lazysubmodules update [<name>...] [--fetch] [--dry-run] [--commit] [--include-prerelease]

Resolve the tracked ref of managed submodules in the local refs, check out
the commit, update .lsm.lock and stage the gitlink, .gitmodules and
.lsm.lock. Without names, every managed submodule is updated.
Uninitialized submodules are initialized, offline when their repository
still exists. Nothing is modified when a selected submodule has
uncommitted changes, a missing ref or needs a clone without --fetch.

Options:
  --commit              create one commit with the result
  --dry-run             print the planned changes and modify nothing
  --fetch               fetch first; clone uninitialized submodules if needed
  --include-prerelease  let tag patterns select pre-release tags

$ lazysubmodules status -h
Usage: lazysubmodules status [<name>...] [--porcelain=v1]

Show the tracking configuration, the lock entry, the checked-out commit and
the state of submodules: ok, behind, drift, dirty, uninitialized,
missing-ref or unmanaged. Without names, every submodule is shown,
including unmanaged ones. Only local refs are consulted; run 'fetch' first
to see what the remotes offer.

Options:
  --porcelain[=v1]  print the stable v1 format for scripts instead of a table

$ lazysubmodules fetch -h
Usage: lazysubmodules fetch [<name>...]

Clone and initialize submodules that are not initialized, then run
'git fetch --tags --force --prune origin' in each of them. Tags moved on
the remote replace the local ones, which lets status and verify detect
them. Without names, every managed submodule is fetched. Git's progress
output goes to standard error.

$ lazysubmodules verify -h
Usage: lazysubmodules verify [<name>...] [--signatures]

Compare the lock commit, the gitlink in the HEAD commit of the superproject
and the submodule HEAD. For tags and tag patterns, also check that the
locked tag still resolves to the locked commit. Every failed check is
printed, and the exit status is 4 when any check fails. Without names,
every managed submodule is verified.

Options:
  --signatures  also verify the signature of the locked tag or commit

$ lazysubmodules foreach -h
Usage: lazysubmodules foreach -- <command> [args...]

Run a command, without a shell, in every managed submodule that is checked
out, in .gitmodules order. Uninitialized submodules are skipped with a note.
The first command that fails ends foreach with exit status 1. The command
gets the variables name, sm_path, displaypath, sha1, toplevel, LSM_MODE and
LSM_REF. The '--' may be left out when the command does not start with '-'.

$ lazysubmodules tui -h
Usage: lazysubmodules tui

Start the terminal user interface. Standard input and output must be a
terminal that supports cursor movement (TERM set and not "dumb").
Git runs without the terminal there, so fetching cannot answer prompts: it
needs a credential helper or an SSH agent, and SSH host keys that are
already known.

$ lazysubmodules version -h
Usage: lazysubmodules version

Print the version, the source commit and the commit date. Values that
the build did not record are printed as "dev", "none" and "unknown".

$ lazysubmodules help -h
Usage: lazysubmodules help [<command>]

Show the list of commands, or the help of one command.

